  "response": "Authorship disputes are settled not by a single arbiter but within an institutional apparatus whose own verdicts remain open to revision. Catalogues raisonn\u00e9s record the body of works accepted as by a given artist, and the Rembrandt Research Project reattributed numerous paintings previously accepted as works by Rembrandt; a fixed documentary register of accepted works cannot be squared with the wholesale reassignment of the very paintings it lists.\n\nAlthough the canon has been reworked on that scale, the two instruments brought to bear on a contested canvas retain their separate competences. Connoisseurship bases attribution on comparison of style, handling, and motif with securely documented works by the artist. Pigment analysis establishes the earliest date at which a painting could have been executed. The asymmetry that matters is one of output: the comparative eye proposes a name, while the laboratory returns a boundary. A familiar configuration shows what happens when the boundary bites. The painting contains a pigment first manufactured after the artist's death; that is, a pigment present in the painting was unavailable during the artist's lifetime. That unavailability indicates that the connoisseurial attribution of the painting to the master is mistaken. The material finding nonetheless leaves two branches open, of which at least one must hold and perhaps both: the attribution of the painting to the master is mistaken; or the sampled paint came from a later restoration campaign rather than from the original layers.\n\nMuch depends, then, on what each technique is competent to establish. Dendrochronology establishes the date of linen canvas supports, whereas radiocarbon dating of a canvas identifies which individual artist painted the work. Misjudging the reach of either procedure is the commonest source of overconfidence in such debates, since a result precise in its own terms may be silent on the question actually at issue.\n\nThe documented history of attribution supplies instructive episodes. Abraham Bredius authenticated van Meegeren's \"Supper at Emmaus\" as an authentic Vermeer. Phenol-formaldehyde resin was detected in the paint films of van Meegeren's forgeries. By contrast with that confident endorsement, the most litigated American case turned on a denial rather than a blessing. Joseph Duveen publicly declared that the Hahn painting was not by Leonardo; as a result of that declaration, the case of Hahn v. Duveen arose from a dispute over a version of Leonardo's \"La Belle Ferronni\u00e8re\". Taken together, these episodes suggest that neither the trained eye nor the analytical instrument is sovereign. A stylistic judgment must answer to the physical record of the object, and a physical finding must be referred back to the question of which layer, campaign, and hand it actually characterizes.",
